{"purpose":"Record that the CLI cannot describe its own flags and output schemas to a machine, forcing agents to scrape help text, and state the acceptance criteria.","content":"## 1. Situation\nAn agent driving eka-cli must currently scrape human help output to learn a command's flags, whether a flag is repeatable, and which `eka-*-v1` schema a result carries. There is no machine-readable description of the command surface.\n\n- `eka manifest --json` exists and describes workspace and repository identity, but not the command surface.\n- `eka schema --json` does not exist (exit 2, unknown command).\n- `--json` coverage is uneven across commands, and list commands are inconsistent about paging.\n\n## 2. Why it matters\nEvery agent session pays discovery cost in tokens and in wrong guesses. A self-describing surface converts that into one cheap call and makes flag errors a validation failure instead of a retry loop. It is also the cheapest possible improvement to agent ergonomics per line of code, because the information already exists in the command definitions; it is merely not exposed.\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria\n- A single command emits machine-readable JSON describing every command: name, synopsis, whether it mutates state, its flags with type, repeatability, default, and required-ness, its arguments, and the schema identifier of its output where one exists.\n- The description is generated from the command definitions, not hand-maintained, so it cannot drift from behaviour.\n- Every read command that produces structured output supports a `--json` form carrying a declared schema identifier.\n- Every list-producing command supports a bound, and its response states whether more results remain.\n- A test asserts that every registered command appears in the description and that no command declares a flag absent from its definition.","appliesTo":["AGENTS.md"]}